# Lesson 08: Variables in C#

A **variable** is a named storage location in memory used to hold data that an application can read and modify during execution. Because C# is a **strongly typed language**, every variable must have a designated type specifying the kind of data it stores, how much memory it occupies, and the operations that can be performed on it.

---

## 1. Variable Declaration and Initialization

In C#, you declare a variable by specifying its **data type** followed by its **identifier (name)**.

```csharp
// Declaration only (allocated, but uninitialized)
int age;

// Initialization (assignment)
age = 25;

// Declaration and initialization in one line (standard practice)
int score = 100;

// Multiple variables of the same type
int x = 10, y = 20, z = 30;

⚠️ Definite Assignment Rule: C# enforces safety by forbidding the read of an uninitialized local variable. Attempting to use a local variable before assigning it a value results in a compile-time error (Use of unassigned local variable).   2. Fundamental Built-in Data TypesTypeCTS EquivalentSizeDescriptionExample LiteralintSystem.Int324 Bytes32-bit signed integer   42   longSystem.Int648 Bytes64-bit signed integer   3000000000L   floatSystem.Single4 BytesSingle-precision floating point (~7 digits)   3.14f   doubleSystem.Double8 BytesDouble-precision floating point (~15-17 digits)   19.99   decimalSystem.Decimal16 BytesHigh-precision financial decimal (28-29 digits)   99.95m   boolSystem.Boolean1 ByteBoolean logical flag   true or false   charSystem.Char2 BytesSingle Unicode UTF-16 character   'A'   stringSystem.StringVariableSequence of Unicode characters   "Hello, .NET"   3. Implicit Typing with varThe var keyword instructs the C# compiler to infer the variable's type automatically based on the value assigned on the right-hand side.   C#var count = 10;           // Compiler infers 'int'
var rate = 4.5;           // Compiler infers 'double'
