Important Rules for var:var is statically typed, not dynamically typed. Once inferred at compile time, the type is fixed and cannot hold values of another type.   The variable must be initialized on the line it is declared so the compiler can infer its type.   Use var when the type is obvious from the right-hand side (e.g., var stream = new MemoryStream();) to reduce code clutter.   4. Constants (const)When a variable's value must remain fixed throughout the lifetime of the program, declare it using the const keyword.   C#const double Pi = 3.14159;
const int MaxRetries = 3;

// MaxRetries = 5; // Compile-time error: The left-hand side of an assignment must be a variable
const fields must be assigned a value at compile time (constants cannot depend on runtime evaluations).   They are immutable and evaluated directly during compilation.   5. Naming Rules & ConventionsIdentifiers must start with a letter (a-z, A-Z) or an underscore (_), followed by letters, digits, or underscores.   C# is case-sensitive (totalScore and TotalScore are different variables).   Keywords cannot be used as variable names unless prefixed with @ (e.g., @class, @int).   Conventions:Use camelCase for local variables and method parameters (e.g., studentId, itemCount).   Use PascalCase for classes, methods, and constants (e.g., CalculateTotal(), TaxRate).   6. Key TakeawaysVariables are named memory slots bound to specific data types.   C# requires local variables to be assigned before being read (Definite Assignment).   Choose numeric types deliberately: int/long for whole numbers, double for general decimals, and decimal for currency and financial calculations[cite: 30].var enables compile-time type inference without losing type safety[cite: 30].Use const for immutable compile-time values[cite: 30].
